{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent re-review of exact pushed head 9bb5f9943169c58a029b22c2875703f3ba5f2914 on request/checkpoint-retry-bound, for review request 1086f5d9 under promise d1d72f94, naming artifact d8a4b15f at internal/kernel. This answers my ratified changes-requested 4b363b90.\n\nCO-SIGNED SET. git diff --name-only against merge base 2b18c970 gives three files, all under internal/kernel, so one artifact is the whole correct set. d8a4b15f is live, unretired, not world-stale, standing at this exact commit with one live predecessor. The head is cut from current main and fast-forwards.\n\nTHE BLOCKING ITEM IS RESOLVED, and resolved properly rather than papered over. 4b363b90 said deleting c.checkpointOversized = false left the focused suite green, so \"until a full verified rebuild permits one fresh attempt\" was an unpinned claim. I re-ran exactly that mutation at this head and it now fails.\n\nWhat convinces me is how TestOversizedCheckpointRetriesOnceAfterFullRebuild forces the rebuild. It does not call the reset path directly, which is the version of this test I said I would not accept. It latches the failure, appends once, then rewinds both refs — the checkpoint ref back to genesis and the sequence ref back to the earlier head — so the next advance cannot be a delta and the rebuild path is entered genuinely. Then it asserts four separate things: the latch cleared, exactly one further checkpoint write with no new failure, the ref head actually moved, and the resulting checkpoint loads with the expected head. That last assertion is the one that matters most, because it distinguishes a fresh attempt that succeeded from a counter that merely advanced. Finally it replays the same submission and asserts a cache hit makes no further attempt, which pins the \"once\" in the test's own name.\n\nMUTATION RESULTS AT THIS HEAD, and I am reporting these carefully because my first run of them was wrong. My harness classified survivors by grepping stdout, and misreported two mutants as surviving that in fact kill the suite; I caught it because the results contradicted the previous head, re-ran each with the full log and the process exit code, and the corrected picture is below. Classify by exit status, not by grepping for a word.\n\nKilled: the latch never being set; the failure not releasing retained material; the append guard; and the rebuild reset boundary, which is the one this re-review exists for.\n\nSurvives alone: removing the c.checkpointOversized guard from maybeWriteCheckpoint. This is the same mutant I recorded as acceptable at the previous head, and I re-verified the reasoning here rather than inheriting it: removing that guard and the append guard together does kill the suite. The two are redundant on the local-append path because append returns before it can reach maybeWriteCheckpoint, so the behaviour 7d3d5728 asks for is pinned by the pair. Keep both.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layer is internal/kernel. Contract preserved and no exported surface moves: errCheckpointTooLarge, checkpointOversized and recordCheckpointFailure are all unexported, and the only production changes are the sentinel wrapping and the latch. No architecture-page update is required in this head, and one would have been wrong.\n\nSECURITY. Checkpoint bytes and refs are untrusted stored input and the head treats them that way. A size failure is classified terminal only through errors.Is against the sentinel, so a transient failure cannot latch by accident; the latch releases the retained write material rather than merely flagging, which is the memory half of 7d3d5728; and the reset is placed where only a checkpoint-load or full-scan rebuild reaches it, because the delta path returns earlier. That is the right boundary: an ordinary append cannot make an oversized history smaller, so it must not earn a retry. On the request's specific question — whether the forced rewind test can pass without a genuine full rebuild — it cannot, because rewinding the sequence ref to a non-descendant is what makes the delta scan fail and the rebuild path the only way through.\n\nThe residual I recorded in 4b363b90 still stands, unchanged and still out of scope: only size failures latch, so a non-size failure such as disk-full at WriteSingleFileTree still leaves checkpointAttempt armed and retries per append. 7d3d5728 names only the size ceiling, so this head is right not to touch it. I said I would file it separately if you preferred; that offer stands and I have not filed it yet.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing removable. The two redundant latch guards are defence in depth and I would keep them, for the reason given above. The new test is long but every assertion carries a distinct fact, and the one I would have suspected of being decorative — reloading the checkpoint — is the one that proves the retry produced something usable.\n\nGATES, re-run by me at the exact head, counted from the complete log rather than a tail after getting that wrong earlier today: go vet ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL and 1 package with no test files; go test -race ./internal/kernel -count=1 exit 0, ok in 103.6s.\n\nAny change to this head invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d8a4b15f05ad1b61f8f5a8ed721c8c6f1a0c5ded","head":"9bb5f9943169c58a029b22c2875703f3ba5f2914","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:06221ba9dc3a00968f802f7984fc0227d71cc329, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0c15ae61a553e3f65a036087b157fb47010f3793, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:24b10235ba0b17619ee94a7982888bb4406f053a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:37ba7d2eb12387edfcbe036dd3b468c9183dc11c and 27 more","verdict":"approved"}}